{"authored" [{"question" "q-181303" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=R\\times N$ in the Cartesian plane?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/state.json" "file_sha256" "ff2d0cddc0a20611d8064b111b2ede906450aaa546e2c4519e4bccc320740bee" "basis" 1445 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181303" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=R\\times N\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a real number and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in N}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(x,1)\\,:\\,x\\in R}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/f8808e4c35775c754dab0bf328a8d129e423009e18ec5f4b962461eba2afcea2.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $2.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(x,2)\\,:\\,x\\in R}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/a51fe9dc5bf7c55b01f72a45d4b88bde4653f0d3da85c1231b0d54c76a6e8c81.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets ${S}_{3},{S}_{4},{S}_{5},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $R\\times N$ as the set of horizontal lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/7ae3c488e60d3dcda304649e575a7d2efc54be7262a54d0455550af420c39b68.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/state.json" "file_sha256" "ff2d0cddc0a20611d8064b111b2ede906450aaa546e2c4519e4bccc320740bee" "basis" 1445 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181303" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "893e98f7a1a12a7185d6615598a046a9738945c4fe39099713070969bc6dafb7" "basis" 1445 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181303" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/state.json" "file_sha256" "ff2d0cddc0a20611d8064b111b2ede906450aaa546e2c4519e4bccc320740bee" "basis" 1445 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-184504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times {1-i}.$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/state.json" "file_sha256" "ff2d0cddc0a20611d8064b111b2ede906450aaa546e2c4519e4bccc320740bee" "basis" 1445 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-184504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[-0.5,0.5]\\times {0.5}={(k,0.5)\\,:\\,k\\in [-0.5,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(-0.5,0.5)$ to the point $(0.5,0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/509d3e8d3bce0b422cad7e7f02377468cacc0de3a4e45647c12fef15f2c9991f.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times {1-i}={(k,1-i)\\,:\\,k\\in [-i,i]}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(-i,1-i)$ to the point $(i,1-i).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/21859c2ac02ba80d47672bf2fab25f7358feb921bc5b546c1e8c783c2819bea3.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/5b5715ba2a1b71a9f5aef52882ed62ba2fe1610c143224914430f79fdc8f06b5.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/state.json" "file_sha256" "ff2d0cddc0a20611d8064b111b2ede906450aaa546e2c4519e4bccc320740bee" "basis" 1445 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-184504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "893e98f7a1a12a7185d6615598a046a9738945c4fe39099713070969bc6dafb7" "basis" 1445 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-184504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/state.json" "file_sha256" "ff2d0cddc0a20611d8064b111b2ede906450aaa546e2c4519e4bccc320740bee" "basis" 1445 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-184504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/119379ee56b15559073a7fefc9100dc8145f376c28271155fc10d9bac7a7594f.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/state.json" "file_sha256" "ff2d0cddc0a20611d8064b111b2ede906450aaa546e2c4519e4bccc320740bee" "basis" 1445 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-184504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/5b5715ba2a1b71a9f5aef52882ed62ba2fe1610c143224914430f79fdc8f06b5.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/state.json" "file_sha256" "ff2d0cddc0a20611d8064b111b2ede906450aaa546e2c4519e4bccc320740bee" "basis" 1445 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-184504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/714c5db9aceb568a4988ce38a32e73a186178939fe0ba48cd384b3cf88f0e937.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/state.json" "file_sha256" "ff2d0cddc0a20611d8064b111b2ede906450aaa546e2c4519e4bccc320740bee" "basis" 1445 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-184504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/c9ba946ca63983aece16f5243680fe8cc7dddf930f4d284fd50b7b2fbb6255dc.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/state.json" "file_sha256" "ff2d0cddc0a20611d8064b111b2ede906450aaa546e2c4519e4bccc320740bee" "basis" 1445 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-184504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/f6d06dbdcf69c4dba1d0375bbe204151c7a5d8098f7385220f3c66f35f7fa0a8.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/state.json" "file_sha256" "ff2d0cddc0a20611d8064b111b2ede906450aaa546e2c4519e4bccc320740bee" "basis" 1445 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-184504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/5b5715ba2a1b71a9f5aef52882ed62ba2fe1610c143224914430f79fdc8f06b5.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/state.json" "file_sha256" "ff2d0cddc0a20611d8064b111b2ede906450aaa546e2c4519e4bccc320740bee" "basis" 1445 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-177649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[-1,1]\\times [-i,i].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/state.json" "file_sha256" "51eb8203b773dd9a7ce3d50db8b3e8b59a5d26c39bb2ec71e413dea433212cc1" "basis" 1687 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-177649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[-1,1]\\times [-0.5,0.5]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-0.5,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(1,-0.5),$ $(1,0.5),$ $(-1,0.5),$ and $(-1,-0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/f0a530434b8ac38fdb4cb517f22e121892c8b7c635c702c1cd9fccb2961aee18.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-1,1]\\times [-i,i]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-i,i]}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(1,-i),$ $(1,i),$ $(-1,i),$ and $(-1,-i).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/6982658bd8ce0e3c81953309ed9f2a70d1e56bbfce8af8862a32da66bf19e638.png)\n\n \n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $[-1,1]\\times {0}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/34a3addbdcf3d5348805f494ed2e427f21f6ad424b0d9d2a411dae9f99b2fb7a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/state.json" "file_sha256" "51eb8203b773dd9a7ce3d50db8b3e8b59a5d26c39bb2ec71e413dea433212cc1" "basis" 1687 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-177649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "938c0b51197a493b9cb23ba1ad10367992afe167a29d072043021c14e5f339a5" "basis" 1687 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-177649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/state.json" "file_sha256" "51eb8203b773dd9a7ce3d50db8b3e8b59a5d26c39bb2ec71e413dea433212cc1" "basis" 1687 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-177649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/0d4597773001223e64f19e1db23c441cc7d4ffb1481c564b63f456fa05e4a0ed.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/state.json" "file_sha256" "51eb8203b773dd9a7ce3d50db8b3e8b59a5d26c39bb2ec71e413dea433212cc1" "basis" 1687 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-177649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/33c6eba539c7bc77e4c349c444da281fb3945aeb9f48018986e8c6373bc0e3b5.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/state.json" "file_sha256" "51eb8203b773dd9a7ce3d50db8b3e8b59a5d26c39bb2ec71e413dea433212cc1" "basis" 1687 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-177649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/34a3addbdcf3d5348805f494ed2e427f21f6ad424b0d9d2a411dae9f99b2fb7a.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/state.json" "file_sha256" "51eb8203b773dd9a7ce3d50db8b3e8b59a5d26c39bb2ec71e413dea433212cc1" "basis" 1687 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-177649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/ebd57b72a8e825778bce33f84bcbec2a0bd8d852a5b2ce25a707224b39d07f99.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/state.json" "file_sha256" "51eb8203b773dd9a7ce3d50db8b3e8b59a5d26c39bb2ec71e413dea433212cc1" "basis" 1687 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-177649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$∅,$ the empty set"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/state.json" "file_sha256" "51eb8203b773dd9a7ce3d50db8b3e8b59a5d26c39bb2ec71e413dea433212cc1" "basis" 1687 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-177649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/34a3addbdcf3d5348805f494ed2e427f21f6ad424b0d9d2a411dae9f99b2fb7a.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/state.json" "file_sha256" "51eb8203b773dd9a7ce3d50db8b3e8b59a5d26c39bb2ec71e413dea433212cc1" "basis" 1687 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}$ in the Cartesian plane?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/state.json" "file_sha256" "51eb8203b773dd9a7ce3d50db8b3e8b59a5d26c39bb2ec71e413dea433212cc1" "basis" 1687 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a negative integer and whose $y$-coordinate is a negative integer.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {Z}^{-},\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is negative integer.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/37ad5627c3fb7cc89e751bb697e01ac7fecaf2712e230b6e3812ad9137d5cd46.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{-2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative integer.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-2}={(-2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/97ebcac85477b8e88564ab67d59c926d5faa72138c8a31d80d3b89895f33668c.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…{S}_{-5},{S}_{-4},{S}_{-3}$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…{S}_{-5}\\cup {S}_{-4}\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1} \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in {Z}^{-}}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize ${Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/a756e53554cb9184d96a52b83038fbd6b00b21d0a12cc658aebb5010e917fd03.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/state.json" "file_sha256" "51eb8203b773dd9a7ce3d50db8b3e8b59a5d26c39bb2ec71e413dea433212cc1" "basis" 1687 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "938c0b51197a493b9cb23ba1ad10367992afe167a29d072043021c14e5f339a5" "basis" 1687 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/state.json" "file_sha256" "51eb8203b773dd9a7ce3d50db8b3e8b59a5d26c39bb2ec71e413dea433212cc1" "basis" 1687 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/2df87b90d770a5f2c0ba40a55330409d1cd80866b7e34299eab6dcbf675c6a13.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/state.json" "file_sha256" "51eb8203b773dd9a7ce3d50db8b3e8b59a5d26c39bb2ec71e413dea433212cc1" "basis" 1687 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/35b2f7b26a964f8892ba85bcdeb4fae5312d515044e355cc6a1f989efc8a825c.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/state.json" "file_sha256" "51eb8203b773dd9a7ce3d50db8b3e8b59a5d26c39bb2ec71e413dea433212cc1" "basis" 1687 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/3ea69cc945f29707432415ed194c8abdec6b60056c8c64c845f27b3139cbd3d8.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/state.json" "file_sha256" "51eb8203b773dd9a7ce3d50db8b3e8b59a5d26c39bb2ec71e413dea433212cc1" "basis" 1687 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/a756e53554cb9184d96a52b83038fbd6b00b21d0a12cc658aebb5010e917fd03.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/state.json" "file_sha256" "51eb8203b773dd9a7ce3d50db8b3e8b59a5d26c39bb2ec71e413dea433212cc1" "basis" 1687 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/e3550a78d58d91efb3eb012cfad691ee22b7bd216de7910850a289db3ab9e2b2.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/state.json" "file_sha256" "51eb8203b773dd9a7ce3d50db8b3e8b59a5d26c39bb2ec71e413dea433212cc1" "basis" 1687 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-177648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times [0,1].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/state.json" "file_sha256" "e8a783b90d8521fbb0edc9914a452f6260567c9f2ea3af81370988be38da1788"} {"question" "q-177648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[0,0.5]\\times [0,1]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,0.5],b\\in [0,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(0.5,0),$ $(0.5,1),$ and $(0,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/5b882f3fb0e7b44eb2df3a510260574e5c4a4d85842a9c0d65515f1a40894756.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times [0,1]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,i],b\\in [0,1]}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(i,0),$ $(i,1),$ and $(0,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/cb530698591dbd71a8058d9faa0c60b636dc654f21e92d65ec8295aeaa67355a.png)\n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment ${0}\\times [0,1]:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/704f8f6f0666d09bb2b8a25eab0548c3eb808e2c9d394d2ae873de35cb0112d1.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/state.json" "file_sha256" "e8a783b90d8521fbb0edc9914a452f6260567c9f2ea3af81370988be38da1788"} {"question" "q-177648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "75f40ef7b022f132889746b23eb42e33ee9f9d8064bd96a21ed157929256f768"} {"question" "q-177648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/state.json" "file_sha256" "e8a783b90d8521fbb0edc9914a452f6260567c9f2ea3af81370988be38da1788"} {"question" "q-177648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/39db7bc1b259b222a2365441babc3421101961ea26a7143af14b526ba7f81200.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/8627676ae3b7bcdeff423d79d7289d1d3f9243e0cc69d4f3ac7d3e5c51141fc1.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/c87871c50b343792c73fc18dfe92bc2065b18ed3de1e7bb70324b8b43d027206.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/ec0e1df36bf5d93b8cdbcc3d14cd510fbe299b929ed3d19f4d901f4461093305.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/f90de7c6d88b187af3604912a647620fb88d4833e08fd086cf1a9daae52f331f.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/state.json" "file_sha256" "e8a783b90d8521fbb0edc9914a452f6260567c9f2ea3af81370988be38da1788"} {"question" "q-177648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/39db7bc1b259b222a2365441babc3421101961ea26a7143af14b526ba7f81200.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/state.json" "file_sha256" "e8a783b90d8521fbb0edc9914a452f6260567c9f2ea3af81370988be38da1788"} {"question" "q-177649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[-1,1]\\times [-i,i].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/state.json" "file_sha256" "e8a783b90d8521fbb0edc9914a452f6260567c9f2ea3af81370988be38da1788"} {"question" "q-177649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[-1,1]\\times [-0.5,0.5]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-0.5,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(1,-0.5),$ $(1,0.5),$ $(-1,0.5),$ and $(-1,-0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/f0a530434b8ac38fdb4cb517f22e121892c8b7c635c702c1cd9fccb2961aee18.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-1,1]\\times [-i,i]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-i,i]}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(1,-i),$ $(1,i),$ $(-1,i),$ and $(-1,-i).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/6982658bd8ce0e3c81953309ed9f2a70d1e56bbfce8af8862a32da66bf19e638.png)\n\n \n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $[-1,1]\\times {0}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/34a3addbdcf3d5348805f494ed2e427f21f6ad424b0d9d2a411dae9f99b2fb7a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/state.json" "file_sha256" "e8a783b90d8521fbb0edc9914a452f6260567c9f2ea3af81370988be38da1788"} {"question" "q-177649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "75f40ef7b022f132889746b23eb42e33ee9f9d8064bd96a21ed157929256f768"} {"question" "q-177649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/state.json" "file_sha256" "e8a783b90d8521fbb0edc9914a452f6260567c9f2ea3af81370988be38da1788"} {"question" "q-177649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/0d4597773001223e64f19e1db23c441cc7d4ffb1481c564b63f456fa05e4a0ed.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/33c6eba539c7bc77e4c349c444da281fb3945aeb9f48018986e8c6373bc0e3b5.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/34a3addbdcf3d5348805f494ed2e427f21f6ad424b0d9d2a411dae9f99b2fb7a.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/ebd57b72a8e825778bce33f84bcbec2a0bd8d852a5b2ce25a707224b39d07f99.png"] ["text" "$∅,$ the empty set"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/state.json" "file_sha256" "e8a783b90d8521fbb0edc9914a452f6260567c9f2ea3af81370988be38da1788"} {"question" "q-177649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/34a3addbdcf3d5348805f494ed2e427f21f6ad424b0d9d2a411dae9f99b2fb7a.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/state.json" "file_sha256" "e8a783b90d8521fbb0edc9914a452f6260567c9f2ea3af81370988be38da1788"} {"question" "q-181303" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=R\\times N$ in the Cartesian plane?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/state.json" "file_sha256" "e8a783b90d8521fbb0edc9914a452f6260567c9f2ea3af81370988be38da1788"} {"question" "q-181303" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=R\\times N\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a real number and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in N}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(x,1)\\,:\\,x\\in R}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/f8808e4c35775c754dab0bf328a8d129e423009e18ec5f4b962461eba2afcea2.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $2.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(x,2)\\,:\\,x\\in R}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/a51fe9dc5bf7c55b01f72a45d4b88bde4653f0d3da85c1231b0d54c76a6e8c81.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets ${S}_{3},{S}_{4},{S}_{5},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $R\\times N$ as the set of horizontal lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/7ae3c488e60d3dcda304649e575a7d2efc54be7262a54d0455550af420c39b68.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/state.json" "file_sha256" "e8a783b90d8521fbb0edc9914a452f6260567c9f2ea3af81370988be38da1788"} {"question" "q-181303" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "75f40ef7b022f132889746b23eb42e33ee9f9d8064bd96a21ed157929256f768"} {"question" "q-181303" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/state.json" "file_sha256" "e8a783b90d8521fbb0edc9914a452f6260567c9f2ea3af81370988be38da1788"} {"question" "q-181303" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/0a03f883f977e67b9299d52e1572e484ed6010dc4f2555c7cbc2048f193dbc7f.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/179fea73a29a658124583602bf26485b25ba70f66ca8187c47d745f46de25e1d.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/89ac9e71d8152097657f0c01daecf2b894dc142db4ba27fbfadcb8ded6e9cb19.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/acccce074e5dee93bbca149c16c5f7794dce17d1cd16432d25b0132382d877f8.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/b8a268a69a978e7f9a14afd83c7c14735d797d4359826ae86f84873e53635363.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/state.json" "file_sha256" "e8a783b90d8521fbb0edc9914a452f6260567c9f2ea3af81370988be38da1788"} {"question" "q-181303" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/b8a268a69a978e7f9a14afd83c7c14735d797d4359826ae86f84873e53635363.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/state.json" "file_sha256" "e8a783b90d8521fbb0edc9914a452f6260567c9f2ea3af81370988be38da1788"} {"question" "q-181979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}$ in the Cartesian plane?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/state.json" "file_sha256" "e8a783b90d8521fbb0edc9914a452f6260567c9f2ea3af81370988be38da1788"} {"question" "q-181979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a negative integer and whose $y$-coordinate is a negative integer.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {Z}^{-},\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is negative integer.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/37ad5627c3fb7cc89e751bb697e01ac7fecaf2712e230b6e3812ad9137d5cd46.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{-2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative integer.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-2}={(-2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/97ebcac85477b8e88564ab67d59c926d5faa72138c8a31d80d3b89895f33668c.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…{S}_{-5},{S}_{-4},{S}_{-3}$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…{S}_{-5}\\cup {S}_{-4}\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1} \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in {Z}^{-}}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize ${Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/a756e53554cb9184d96a52b83038fbd6b00b21d0a12cc658aebb5010e917fd03.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/state.json" "file_sha256" "e8a783b90d8521fbb0edc9914a452f6260567c9f2ea3af81370988be38da1788"} {"question" "q-181979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "75f40ef7b022f132889746b23eb42e33ee9f9d8064bd96a21ed157929256f768"} {"question" "q-181979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/state.json" "file_sha256" "e8a783b90d8521fbb0edc9914a452f6260567c9f2ea3af81370988be38da1788"} {"question" "q-181979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/2df87b90d770a5f2c0ba40a55330409d1cd80866b7e34299eab6dcbf675c6a13.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/35b2f7b26a964f8892ba85bcdeb4fae5312d515044e355cc6a1f989efc8a825c.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/3ea69cc945f29707432415ed194c8abdec6b60056c8c64c845f27b3139cbd3d8.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/a756e53554cb9184d96a52b83038fbd6b00b21d0a12cc658aebb5010e917fd03.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/e3550a78d58d91efb3eb012cfad691ee22b7bd216de7910850a289db3ab9e2b2.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/state.json" "file_sha256" "e8a783b90d8521fbb0edc9914a452f6260567c9f2ea3af81370988be38da1788"} {"question" "q-181979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/a756e53554cb9184d96a52b83038fbd6b00b21d0a12cc658aebb5010e917fd03.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/state.json" "file_sha256" "e8a783b90d8521fbb0edc9914a452f6260567c9f2ea3af81370988be38da1788"} {"question" "q-184504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times {1-i}.$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/state.json" "file_sha256" "e8a783b90d8521fbb0edc9914a452f6260567c9f2ea3af81370988be38da1788"} {"question" "q-184504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[-0.5,0.5]\\times {0.5}={(k,0.5)\\,:\\,k\\in [-0.5,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(-0.5,0.5)$ to the point $(0.5,0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/509d3e8d3bce0b422cad7e7f02377468cacc0de3a4e45647c12fef15f2c9991f.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times {1-i}={(k,1-i)\\,:\\,k\\in [-i,i]}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(-i,1-i)$ to the point $(i,1-i).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/21859c2ac02ba80d47672bf2fab25f7358feb921bc5b546c1e8c783c2819bea3.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/5b5715ba2a1b71a9f5aef52882ed62ba2fe1610c143224914430f79fdc8f06b5.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/state.json" "file_sha256" "e8a783b90d8521fbb0edc9914a452f6260567c9f2ea3af81370988be38da1788"} {"question" "q-184504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "75f40ef7b022f132889746b23eb42e33ee9f9d8064bd96a21ed157929256f768"} {"question" "q-184504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/state.json" "file_sha256" "e8a783b90d8521fbb0edc9914a452f6260567c9f2ea3af81370988be38da1788"} {"question" "q-184504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/119379ee56b15559073a7fefc9100dc8145f376c28271155fc10d9bac7a7594f.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/5b5715ba2a1b71a9f5aef52882ed62ba2fe1610c143224914430f79fdc8f06b5.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/714c5db9aceb568a4988ce38a32e73a186178939fe0ba48cd384b3cf88f0e937.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/c9ba946ca63983aece16f5243680fe8cc7dddf930f4d284fd50b7b2fbb6255dc.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/f6d06dbdcf69c4dba1d0375bbe204151c7a5d8098f7385220f3c66f35f7fa0a8.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/state.json" "file_sha256" "e8a783b90d8521fbb0edc9914a452f6260567c9f2ea3af81370988be38da1788"} {"question" "q-184504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/5b5715ba2a1b71a9f5aef52882ed62ba2fe1610c143224914430f79fdc8f06b5.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/state.json" "file_sha256" "e8a783b90d8521fbb0edc9914a452f6260567c9f2ea3af81370988be38da1788"}] "usage" {"q-177649" [] "q-181979" [] "q-181303" [] "q-184504" []} "basis" 2869 "retractions" [[17592186325703 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[-1,1]\\times [-0.5,0.5]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-0.5,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(1,-0.5),$ $(1,0.5),$ $(-1,0.5),$ and $(-1,-0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/f0a530434b8ac38fdb4cb517f22e121892c8b7c635c702c1cd9fccb2961aee18.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-1,1]\\times [-i,i]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-i,i]}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(1,-i),$ $(1,i),$ $(-1,i),$ and $(-1,-i).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/6982658bd8ce0e3c81953309ed9f2a70d1e56bbfce8af8862a32da66bf19e638.png)\n\n \n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $[-1,1]\\times {0}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/34a3addbdcf3d5348805f494ed2e427f21f6ad424b0d9d2a411dae9f99b2fb7a.png)"] [17592186198705 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=R\\times N\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a real number and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in N}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(x,1)\\,:\\,x\\in R}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/f8808e4c35775c754dab0bf328a8d129e423009e18ec5f4b962461eba2afcea2.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $2.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(x,2)\\,:\\,x\\in R}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/a51fe9dc5bf7c55b01f72a45d4b88bde4653f0d3da85c1231b0d54c76a6e8c81.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets ${S}_{3},{S}_{4},{S}_{5},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $R\\times N$ as the set of horizontal lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/7ae3c488e60d3dcda304649e575a7d2efc54be7262a54d0455550af420c39b68.png)"] [17592186325724 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a negative integer and whose $y$-coordinate is a negative integer.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {Z}^{-},\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is negative integer.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/37ad5627c3fb7cc89e751bb697e01ac7fecaf2712e230b6e3812ad9137d5cd46.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{-2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative integer.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-2}={(-2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/97ebcac85477b8e88564ab67d59c926d5faa72138c8a31d80d3b89895f33668c.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…{S}_{-5},{S}_{-4},{S}_{-3}$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…{S}_{-5}\\cup {S}_{-4}\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1} \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in {Z}^{-}}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize ${Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/a756e53554cb9184d96a52b83038fbd6b00b21d0a12cc658aebb5010e917fd03.png)"] [17592186308023 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[-0.5,0.5]\\times {0.5}={(k,0.5)\\,:\\,k\\in [-0.5,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(-0.5,0.5)$ to the point $(0.5,0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/509d3e8d3bce0b422cad7e7f02377468cacc0de3a4e45647c12fef15f2c9991f.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times {1-i}={(k,1-i)\\,:\\,k\\in [-i,i]}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(-i,1-i)$ to the point $(i,1-i).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/21859c2ac02ba80d47672bf2fab25f7358feb921bc5b546c1e8c783c2819bea3.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/5b5715ba2a1b71a9f5aef52882ed62ba2fe1610c143224914430f79fdc8f06b5.png)"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186195212 17592186195213 17592186195214 17592186195215 17592186195216 17592186308025 17592186308026 17592186308027 17592186308028 17592186308029 17592186325705 17592186325706 17592186325707 17592186325708 17592186325709 17592186325726 17592186325727 17592186325728 17592186325729 17592186325730] "immutable_fields" [[17592186195211 :answer-field/correct] [17592186195211 :answer-field/id] [17592186195211 :answer-field/key] [17592186195211 :answer-field/type] [17592186308024 :answer-field/correct] [17592186308024 :answer-field/id] [17592186308024 :answer-field/key] [17592186308024 :answer-field/type] [17592186325704 :answer-field/correct] [17592186325704 :answer-field/id] [17592186325704 :answer-field/key] [17592186325704 :answer-field/type] [17592186325725 :answer-field/correct] [17592186325725 :answer-field/id] [17592186325725 :answer-field/key] [17592186325725 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
